1785468177554073	agentbus-dev	itc:v3:AWLJM_JJknJ8zQAAAAAAQdW_VauvtgAAAAEvBg		item.assign	bn-1wg	{"action":"assign","agent":"agentbus-dev"}	blake3:dSSu2GPPGptlxc0z02N8S45Go2skbpqmBX2lPLYwuHs
1785468545084939	agentbus-dev	itc:v3:AWLJM_JJknJ8zQAAAAAAQdW_VauvtgAAAAEvBw		item.comment	bn-x7r	{"body":"Fixed. register_session() no longer returns early when location() finds no pane -- the record is always appended, with mux/mux_session/pane empty. pid, starttime and transcript are preserved, which is exactly what the early return was throwing away.\n\nAlso fixed the same silent drop in state(), which carried an identical `if pane.is_empty() { return; }`. A report that names its own session is now written pane or no pane: \"blocked\" reaches no transcript, so a hook report is the only way it is ever heard, and a vessel-hosted agent hitting a permission prompt was reporting nothing at all. The one case still dropped is a report with neither a session id nor a pane -- the synthesised \"pane:\" id needs a pane to name it after, so such a report is about nothing.\n\nConsumer side (main.rs): a location-less registration could shadow an inbox report that DID know the pane, since the merge was or_insert_with. It now fills the location in from the report while keeping the transcript that only the registration has.\n\nVerified: hook register with all four mux env vars unset writes {\"mux\":\"\",\"mux_session\":\"\",\"pane\":\"\",\"pid\":760563,\"starttime\":21744155,\"transcript\":\"...\"}; inside zellij unchanged; a subagent payload still declines. Folding a location-less registration yields an empty location and nothing else changed, and name_subagents can now reach the subagent sidecars through its transcript."}	blake3:in8KEuZmDNrvPg0Z35Jew-Cx-x5nt9xLsugnImIQaR0
1785468553625618	agentbus-dev	itc:v3:AWLJM_JJknJ8zQAAAAAAQdW_VauvtgAAAAEvCA		item.comment	bn-1vj	{"body":"Fixed by carrying the answer, not by documenting the asymmetry -- the normaliser exists so subscribers never special-case a source.\n\nclaude.rs now holds a per-transcript `Turn { answer }`. Every assistant text block updates it (clamped with one_line(.., 160), the same clamp codex.rs applies to last_agent_message, so the log line stays atomically appendable). turn_duration takes it: `result: turn.answer.take()`. Taken rather than read, so a turn that says nothing reports nothing instead of repeating the previous turn edge case is real: an interrupted turn never gets a turn_duration, so its half-answer is also cleared at the next prompt.\n\nState lives in main.rs Stream, alongside the other per-file context, so normalize() stays a function of one line plus what the stream remembers.\n\nMeasured on a real 3.8k-line transcript replayed through `agentbus events`: 74 turn_end events, 74 with a non-null result. Before this, all 74 were null. Sample pairing now reads prompt \"This is a new, exploratory project...\" -> turn_end \"Research done. Here's what I found. ## The key disco...\"."}	blake3:q3m6CDlFZj5ycUxkP2e-Y0Q9VryJwS1O5a5E1eXM4Ig
1785468568142129	agentbus-dev	itc:v3:AWLJM_JJknJ8zQAAAAAAQdW_VauvtgAAAAEvCQ		item.comment	bn-1vj	{"body":"Correction to the garbled sentence above: it should read \"...so a turn that says nothing reports nothing instead of repeating the previous turn's answer. That edge case is real: an interrupted turn never gets a turn_duration, so its half-answer is also cleared at the next prompt.\""}	blake3:PnDH9iCniNg8Ko8UaVWKx0ZfkywKsrbdmlmNKW67a0Q
1785468568157889	agentbus-dev	itc:v3:AWLJM_JJknJ8zQAAAAAAQdW_VauvtgAAAAEvCg		item.comment	bn-1wg	{"body":"Fixed by emitting Kind::Prompt from the transcript's `user` records -- option 1, and the one that needs no vocabulary change. Prompt is already documented as \"doubles as the start-of-turn signal\" and is what codex.rs emits, so both sources now bracket a turn as prompt -> turn_end and the wait loop that hung (\"see a prompt, then wait for turn_end\") works unchanged against either. last-prompt stays a Label; its reasoning was sound and is untouched.\n\nThe whole difficulty is telling a prompt from the many `user` records that are not one. user_prompt() rejects, in order: isMeta (local-command caveats, pasted-image placeholders), isSidechain, any message containing a tool_result block, empty text, text starting with \"<\" (slash-command machinery -- <command-name>, <local-command-stdout> -- the same line codex.rs draws around its replayed context blocks), and \"[Request interrupted by user...\", which is what escape writes immediately before the real prompt.\n\nMeasured on a real 3.8k-line transcript: 801 tool_result user records produced 0 prompt events, and the /effort burst (caveat + <command-name> + <local-command-stdout>) produced none either. 76 prompts against 74 turn_ends, the difference being interrupted turns and the unfinished final one. Nothing matching ^(<|\\[Request interrupted) leaked into a prompt.\n\nVisible live: this session's snapshot row now carries its label while the turn is still running. Previously the label only appeared once last-prompt was written, i.e. after the turn had already ended."}	blake3:I-uk9EeBVrSSs-jWeZ-HRkepsbKKnEr_rf_Gd2OHf_g
1785468570740008	agentbus-dev	itc:v3:AWLJM_JJknJ8zQAAAAAAQdW_VauvtgAAAAEvCw		item.move	bn-x7r	{"state":"done"}	blake3:cqao_vOQHzMQjoeXidPVn3wJOk4rDdrVuk9U0CLPukM
1785468570743310	agentbus-dev	itc:v3:AWLJM_JJknJ8zQAAAAAAQdW_VauvtgAAAAEvDA		item.move	bn-1vj	{"state":"done"}	blake3:jSA99btwHo31kTCjEqelC7bBFCA6xywnzfD03qH-Z-s
1785468570743884	agentbus-dev	itc:v3:AWLJM_JJknJ8zQAAAAAAQdW_VauvtgAAAAEvDQ		item.move	bn-1wg	{"state":"done"}	blake3:em5caugmKWfW87MbmpUOou52Yaq_Ipo_xHkvNvKCs-M
1785474353587014	skills-dev	itc:v3:AWLJk_5k8kmTJEAAAAAAQda_7XqtagAAAAEwBA		item.create	bn-1uj	{"description":"to_json() still emits {\"version\": 1} after the session shape changed\nincompatibly: the old\n\n    \"pane\": {\"zellij_session\": \"agents\", \"pane_id\": \"4\"}\n\nbecame\n\n    \"location\": {\"mux\": \"zellij\", \"session\": \"agents\", \"pane\": \"4\"}\n\nThe rename is an improvement -- 'mux' is exactly right now that tmux, wezterm and\nkitty are recognised, and a subscriber has to know which one it is looking at.\nThe issue is only that the version field did not move with it.\n\nHow it fails for a subscriber: reading s['pane']['pane_id'] against the new\nsnapshot yields nothing, so no session ever matches a pane. Nothing errors --\nthe consumer simply never binds, and every wait reports a timeout with the agent\nsitting there having already answered. It took a schema dump to find, because\nthe failure looks like the agent not responding rather than like a parse error.\n\nBumping to 2 would let a subscriber say 'I understand 1 and 2' and warn on\nanything else, rather than mis-binding silently. The field is already published\nand nothing appears to read it yet, so this is nearly free right now and gets\nmore expensive with every subscriber written against it.\n\nWorth considering alongside: the same release added state_since, tools and\neffort. Those are additive and safe for an existing consumer, which is the\ndistinction a version number is there to express.","kind":"task","size":"xs","title":"Snapshot schema version stayed at 1 across a breaking rename"}	blake3:PZocZXQ_CSkNlfGtnd7dJTM3U0mXYDPBdZVAhEGISJM
1785475202642529	skills-dev	itc:v3:AWLJk_5k8kmTJEAAAAAAQda_7XqtagAAAAEwBQ		item.create	bn-1eo	{"description":"The verb a consumer actually wants, and the one whose absence costs the most.\n\nToday a subscriber that needs \"tell me when this agent is done\" must locate the\nsnapshot, poll it, notice a working->idle transition, avoid returning the\n*previous* turn's answer, and then go find the answer itself. In the\nvessel-agents skill that machinery measured 145 lines — 18% of the script — and\nnone of it is domain logic. It is bookkeeping agentbus is far better placed to\ndo, being already a daemon tailing the event log.\n\nProposed:\n\n    agentbus wait --session S | --pid N [--timeout SECS] [--json]\n\nBlocks until the current-or-next turn for that session ends, prints\n{status, session, result, duration_ms}, exits non-zero on anything but done.\n\nThe semantics that matter, roughly in order of how easy they are to get wrong:\n\n- **It must not return a turn that ended before the call started.** This is the\n  whole difficulty. A caller polling \"is it idle\" between submitting a prompt\n  and the agent picking it up sees the *previous* turn's idle and takes the\n  previous turn's answer. Measured: a trivial codex turn completes in 1.1s, so a\n  1s poll can straddle an entire turn. Internally this is a watermark on the\n  event log taken at call entry — cheap here, and impossible for a caller to do\n  correctly across two processes without persisting it to a file, which is\n  exactly what the skill does today.\n\n- **\"blocked\" must be a distinct terminal status, not a timeout.** An agent\n  sitting on a permission prompt is indistinguishable from a slow one to any\n  screen-based waiter and burns the caller's entire timeout. Hooks already\n  report the state; only this verb can surface it promptly.\n\n- **Current-or-next, not next.** A caller that submits and then waits has\n  already started the turn and must not hang waiting for another one.\n\n- Timeout should exit distinguishably from blocked, and say which.\n\nDepends on the same pid -> session resolution as the sessions query bone, and is\nmost useful once a turn's result is the full answer rather than the 160-char\npreview — both filed separately.\n\nA streaming sibling (agentbus watch --session S emitting state transitions as\nJSONL) would serve supervisors following several agents without one process per\nagent, but this blocking single-shot form removes the most consumer code and\nshould come first.","kind":"task","size":"m","title":"agentbus wait: block until a turn ends, and say what it said"}	blake3:Y1J9dIrq_adZEF4EUuwhB2IsKOiSPihvkJPfQuhnXVQ
1785475221838087	skills-dev	itc:v3:AWLJk_5k8kmTJEAAAAAAQda_7XqtagAAAAEwBg		item.create	bn-25h	{"description":"agentbus publishes state but nothing consumes it on a caller's behalf, so every\nsubscriber reimplements the same four things: find the snapshot, parse its\nschema, resolve an identity to a session, and keep up as both change.\n\nIn the vessel-agents skill that is 40% of the script. It is also where the\nbreakage lands: when the session shape went from pane{zellij_session, pane_id}\nto location{mux, session, pane}, the consumer did not error — it silently bound\nnothing and every wait reported a timeout while the agent sat there having\nalready answered.\n\nProposed:\n\n    agentbus sessions [--pid N] [--session S] [--cwd D] [--json]\n\nPrints matching sessions from current state; no filters means all.\n\nTwo things it should own rather than each caller:\n\n- **--pid must match descendants, not just the exact pid.** agentbus registers\n  the *agent* process, which is not always the process a supervisor spawned:\n  codex runs behind a node shim, so vessel reports the shim (1016689) and\n  agentbus records the real binary one level below (1016952); for claude the two\n  coincide. Every consumer otherwise rediscovers this and hand-rolls a\n  /proc/<pid>/stat walk. agentbus already has ppid_of and starttime, and can pair\n  pid with start time to rule out recycling, which a caller racing process exit\n  cannot do as safely.\n\n- **The snapshot's location.** Callers currently hardcode a path and a\n  precedence rule between the zellij tmp dir and the state dir. That is an\n  implementation detail leaking into every subscriber.\n\nThe wider point: the snapshot schema is a public API only because consumers read\nthe file directly. Behind a query verb it stops being one, and the version-bump\nproblem filed separately mostly evaporates — the file becomes an internal\ntransport rather than a contract.\n\nSmall and mechanical compared to the wait bone, and wait wants the same\npid -> session resolution, so this is the natural first piece.","kind":"task","size":"s","title":"agentbus sessions: a query verb, so subscribers stop reading the snapshot file"}	blake3:f-hJHZ8UwZq0AFOw7AsW7Qa3vXRi5h_UaxvfFhNdIls
1785475221838271	skills-dev	itc:v3:AWLJk_5k8kmTJEAAAAAAQda_7XqtagAAAAEwBw		item.link	bn-1eo	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-25h"}	blake3:zTwkusX_Ns39a87O3y05CUKl4DJ9n1dzqLEykWhNTw0
1785475242258963	skills-dev	itc:v3:AWLJk_5k8kmTJEAAAAAAQda_7XqtagAAAAEwCA		item.create	bn-2r7	{"description":"The bus can say a turn ended but not what it said, so every consumer that wants\nthe answer reimplements transcript reading — and does it worse.\n\nturn_end.result is rendered with one_line(text, 160) (src/event.rs): whitespace\nruns collapsed to single spaces, cut at 160 characters with an ellipsis. That is\nright for its consumer, a terminal status line, and the same reasoning explains\nstripping control characters rather than escaping them. But it is a preview, not\na transport. Any consumer preferring it loses every answer longer than a\nsentence along with all paragraph structure.\n\nSo a consumer that wants the real answer must:\n\n1. Find the transcript — register.jsonl when present, otherwise a glob:\n     ~/.claude/projects/*/<sid>.jsonl\n     ~/.codex/sessions/*/*/*/rollout-*-<sid>.jsonl\n2. Sniff which agent wrote it, since a path carries no such label, and handle\n   three record shapes:\n     Claude: type=assistant -> message.content[] with type=text\n     codex:  payload.type=message, role=assistant -> content[] with type=output_text\n     codex:  payload.type=agent_message -> message   (same text, event spelling)\n   while skipping payload.type=reasoning, which is not the answer.\n3. Guard against reading mid-write and returning the *previous* turn's answer.\n   The vessel-agents skill does this by requiring the truncated preview to be a\n   prefix of the transcript text under the same whitespace collapsing — a check\n   that only exists because the two sources disagree.\n\nAll three steps are parsing agentbus already does. The normaliser reads these\nexact records to emit turn_end; it simply discards the full text on the way out.\n\nProposal: carry the untruncated final message. Either as a field alongside the\npreview (result_full, or result plus result_preview), or via\n\n    agentbus result --session S [--turn N]\n\nFolding it into the wait bone's --json output is the shape that removes the most\nconsumer code: one call returns \"the turn ended, and here is what it said\".\n\nWorth deciding explicitly whether the bus is a status feed or a transport. Both\nare defensible; today it reads as the first while consumers need the second, and\nthey pay for the gap in per-agent transcript parsing that drifts every time an\nagent changes its record format.","kind":"task","size":"m","title":"Publish a turn's full answer, not a 160-char status-line preview"}	blake3:dA5Pl4vtdTbBK6fur-awzEzNyKv22gLl2iKVzI0D7OI
1785475242259100	skills-dev	itc:v3:AWLJk_5k8kmTJEAAAAAAQda_7XqtagAAAAEwCQ		item.link	bn-1eo	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-2r7"}	blake3:9lac9deoM0oMIsiIfy4dCRjSuNQJ_0nCiDecB4pO6UY
1785475270960260	agentbus-dev	itc:v3:AWLJM_JJknJ8zQAAAAAAQdW_VauvtgAAAAEvDg		item.create	bn-1mp	{"description":"agentbus observes Claude Code, Codex and OpenCode. Adding agy -- Google's\nAntigravity CLI, installed here as 1.1.8 at ~/.local/bin/agy -- is what puts\nGemini models on the bus, and it is the first host whose transcript is not an\nappend-only file.\n\nWHERE ITS STATE LIVES\n\nOne SQLite database per conversation:\n\n    ~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/conversations/<uuid>.db\n\nThat breaks the assumption tail.rs is built on. Transcripts are ranked first for\ntrust precisely because they can be tailed by byte offset with no agent\ncooperation and no races; a database can't be. Two things soften it:\n\n  - `steps` has a monotonically increasing integer `idx` primary key and a\n    `status` column. That is the moral equivalent of a byte offset, so the\n    cursor-and-resume shape of the existing tailer still applies.\n  - The failure modes are different, not harder: a reader has to cope with WAL\n    mode, a writer holding locks, and a partially written turn, instead of a\n    truncated or replaced file.\n\nThe real cost is the payloads. `step_payload`, `metadata`, `render_info` and\nfriends are protobuf blobs, not JSON, so pulling out prompt text, assistant\ntext, tool calls and turn boundaries needs a schema we do not have. The same\ndirectory still holds an older `.pb` conversation alongside the `.db` files,\nwhich says the format has already changed once and is not a stable contract.\n\nTHE CHEAPER PATH, WHICH SHOULD BE CHECKED FIRST\n\nHooks here are an identity bridge, not a data source -- they exist to report the\none thing no transcript records, which is where a session is. agy appears to\nhave a hook system already: the binary carries `hooks_go_proto.SessionStartHookArgs`\nand an `onSessionStart` handler, and ~/.gemini/settings.json already contains a\n`hooks` block in the same shape Claude and Codex use:\n\n    { \"hooks\": { \"BeforeTool\": [ { \"matcher\": \"...\",\n        \"hooks\": [ { \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"/path/to/bin\" } ] } ] } }\n\nIf agy has SessionStart and UserPromptSubmit equivalents and really does accept\nthat shape, `agentbus hook register` may wire straight in with no new code, and\nthe question of how much of this is work at all turns on that.\n\nUnverified, and worth settling before any protobuf work: the hooks block above\nlives in ~/.gemini/settings.json, which is gemini-cli's file. agy's own settings\nare at ~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/settings.json and carry no `hooks` key, so\nwhich file it actually reads is unknown. `agy plugin import [source]` also\nclaims to import plugins from gemini or claude, and integrations/opencode.js is\nthe precedent if a plugin turns out to be the answer.\n\nSUGGESTED ORDER\n\nSettle the hook path first, because it decides the shape of everything after\nit. If hooks deliver identity and turn boundaries, the SQLite reader is left\nsupplying titles and answer text -- and may not be needed in a first cut at all.\nA host that reports working/idle/blocked with no answer text is already useful\nto a status bar, and is a far smaller bite than reverse-engineering a protobuf\nschema that has already changed once.\n\nOne thing to decide explicitly rather than inherit: whether agy reports a pid.\nA host that reports through an integration instead of a hook publishes none, and\nthen binding falls back to matching on cwd, which is ambiguous the moment two\nagents share a project directory.","kind":"task","size":"l","title":"Observe the agy TUI, to bring Gemini models onto the bus"}	blake3:z9aIEiXQctqI47366_4lNUoJkuS8j1P7dx1_KaRDwmg
1785475535396809	agentbus-dev	itc:v3:AWLJM_JJknJ8zQAAAAAAQdW_VauvtgAAAAEvDw		item.move	bn-1uj	{"state":"done"}	blake3:bYTW93CzCiXA_5N6GuitmIkQdxqGVk-EE5qQD2_gYq8
1785475782180653	skills-dev	itc:v3:AWLJk_5k8kmTJEAAAAAAQda_7XqtagAAAAEwCg		item.create	bn-1f4	{"description":"The verb a consumer actually wants, and the one whose absence costs the most.\n\nToday a subscriber that needs 'tell me when this agent is done' must: locate the\nsnapshot, poll it, notice a working->idle transition, avoid returning the\nPREVIOUS turn's answer, and then go find the answer itself. In the vessel-agents\nskill that machinery measured 145 lines -- 18% of the script -- and none of it is\ndomain logic. It is all bookkeeping that agentbus is better placed to do, since\nit is already a daemon tailing the event log.\n\nProposed:\n\n    agentbus wait --session S | --pid N [--timeout SECS] [--json]\n\nBlocks until the current-or-next turn for that session ends, then prints\n{status, session, result, duration_ms} and exits non-zero on anything but done.\n\nThe semantics that matter, in rough order of how easy they are to get wrong:\n\n  - It must not return a turn that ended BEFORE the call started. This is the\n    whole difficulty. A caller polling 'is it idle' between submitting a prompt\n    and the agent picking it up sees the previous turn's idle and takes the\n    previous turn's answer. Measured window: a trivial codex turn completes in\n    1.1s, so a 1s poll straddles an entire turn. Internally this is a watermark\n    on the event log taken at call entry -- cheap here, and impossible for a\n    caller to do correctly across two processes without persisting it to a file,\n    which is exactly what the skill has to do today.\n\n  -  must be a distinct terminal status, not a timeout. An agent\n    sitting on a permission prompt is indistinguishable from a slow one to any\n    screen-based waiter, and burns the caller's whole timeout. Hooks already\n    report it; only this verb can surface it promptly.\n\n  - 'Current-or-next' rather than 'next': a caller that submits and then waits\n    has already started the turn, and must not hang waiting for another one.\n\n  - Timeout should exit distinguishably from blocked, and say which.\n\nA streaming sibling ( emitting state transitions as\nJSONL) would serve supervisors that follow several agents without one process\nper agent, but the blocking single-shot form is what removes the most consumer\ncode and should come first.","kind":"task","size":"m","title":"agentbus wait: block until a turn ends, and say what it said"}	blake3:PCOwChILCttTlOyp2N8pa1ae-Yrt9Z-EAePOKeBZocM
1785476326176165	agentbus-dev	itc:v3:AWLJM_JJknJ8zQAAAAAAQdW_VauvtgAAAAEvEA		item.move	bn-25h	{"state":"done"}	blake3:KOi7bmwxJgQtq3yy_gXXJMhNDc_b0bikGFnC9wUzhR4
